// **two recoveries over one failure used to eat a closing brace**, and the compiler aborted on an
// assertion with no location at all.
//
// `$x` is not declared, so the call's argument fails; the call recovers past the `;` that ends this
// statement. the `return` around it then found no `;` and recovered a second time - and that skip took
// the function's `}`. from there the body pass was one brace-level deep for the rest of the file, so it
// reached `struct bar` inside the function's lexical scope while the declaration pass, which walks a
// block token by token, had seen it at file scope. two AST nodes for one struct, two field-wise
// constructors at one name token, and AST::FunctionRegistry::claim_declaration_site aborting on the
// second one.
//
// the struct *after* the function is the whole of what makes it visible: a `function` there would have
// been reconciled by find_by_declaration_site, and a struct declared *before* is found by name in either
// scope. what is pinned here is that the file reports the one thing that is actually wrong with it

function f() : bar { return bar($x); }

struct bar { usize $pos; }

echo 1;
